# Summary of changes for run 627af306-75d9-4a81-806a-5b24cfc24048
I've written all five packaging files for the dial cross-talk result, in the project root. I rebuilt none of the proofs. No LaTeX compiler was available, so `RESEARCH_PAPER.tex` has not been compiled, and the two plotting scripts have been syntax-checked but not run.

The central result the files present: take two cubics with Galois group S₃ and read their splitting types (111, 12 or 3) at the same prime. The mutual information between the two readings is exactly 0 bits if their quadratic resolvents differ (combined group S₃ × S₃). It is exactly 1 bit if they share a resolvent (combined group S₃ ×_{C₂} S₃). For semiprimes the values are 0 and 2 bits.

- **`ARTICLE.md`** (about 2,400 words): a popular-science piece that builds from "a cubic is a dial" through Chebotarev and mutual information to the 0-or-1-bit dichotomy. It explains why the small measured values are sampling bias and covers the general one-bit law and the semiprime case.
- **`RESEARCH_PAPER.md`** (about 4,200 words) and **`RESEARCH_PAPER.tex`**, which mirrors it:
  - abstract and modelling assumptions (the Galois group of the combined field, and Chebotarev);
  - the entropy definitions;
  - the general theorems with proof sketches: independence implies zero information, additivity over products, data processing, and at least one bit on a fibre product over a two-element quotient;
  - the exact cubic computations (0 bits, 1 bit, entropy 2/3 + ½·log₂3 for one dial, 4/3 + log₂3 for the pair);
  - algorithms, numerical evidence, discussion and future work.

  Neither version mentions formalisation or code names.
- **`demo.py`** (standard library only, about a minute to run): it reproduces the exact group values (0, 1, 0 and 2 bits, with the predicted entropies). It also runs the experiment on real primes 5 ≤ p < 30000. That gave 0.000243 and 0.000808 bits for the two pairs with different resolvents, against a sampling bias of about 0.00089. It gave 1.000267 and 1.000023 bits for the two shared-resolvent pairs. These prime measurements are statistics, and the documents label them that way.
- **`PACKAGE.json`** bundles all of the above with the metadata (domain "Bridges", dated 2026-09-28) and:
  - one demo entry and two algorithms (the exact calculation on a group, and the prime experiment with bias and shuffle test), each with pseudocode and Python;
  - two plotting scripts: the two joint tables side by side, and how the estimate converges as more primes are used;
  - one interactive page. It switches between the two cases, shows the exact joint table, lets you click a row to condition on one dial, and simulates random primes against the exact value and the bias curve. It can also load the real tables from primes below 30000. Its JavaScript passes a syntax check but I haven't run it in a browser.
  - a guided narrative that places these elements inline, with proofs in expandable sections;
  - the full Phase A proof file (`Catalog/Bridges/DialCrossTalk.lean`) under `lean_files`;
  - the Phase A future directions, lightly edited. The fourth one arrived cut off mid-sentence; I finished it as "zero cross-talk implies the joint table factorises", its evident intended meaning.

One number doesn't reconcile. The headline semiprime figure (0.001424 bits) doesn't match the value in the Phase A notes (0.0216 bits) or my own rerun (0.0243 bits). Both of those sit below the expected sampling bias of about 0.029 bits. The documents quote the headline figure only as reported and don't try to explain the gap.

Everything is committed.
